---
description: Generate architecture visualisations (request flow, load, cost, risk) from this codebase
alwaysApply: false
---

When asked to visualise, diagram, or document this system's architecture:

1. FIRST run `npx infraviz view` and tell the user to open the URL. Do NOT grep,
   read or otherwise explore their codebase before they have accepted the
   data-handling notice shown there. Exploring first is the natural instinct and
   the wrong one — they have not yet seen what will be read or where it goes.
   `npx infraviz spec` refuses to emit the prompts until consent is recorded, so
   you cannot finish the task by skipping this. You also cannot record consent
   yourself — only a human click in the viewer or a typed terminal confirmation
   counts. Show them the notice, then wait.
2. Read the full spec: run `npx infraviz spec`
3. Write `.infraviz/project.json`, then per-service artifacts under
   `.infraviz/services/<id>/`
4. Report progress as you go with `npx infraviz progress "..."` (and `--done` at
   the end) so the user's open viewer shows what you are doing
5. Run `npx infraviz verify` and fix anything it reports
6. Stop after the scan and report — do not analyse every service unasked

Non-negotiable: every finding must include `file` plus a `fingerprint` — an exact
substring that really exists in that file. Verification re-checks these against
disk, so invented citations are caught and shown as unverified.

Returning zero findings for a simple service is correct. Do not pad.
